--TEST--
`Core\Db::open` over § 2's SQLite arm asks `db.open` about the *path*, because that arm has no host for a host-shaped grant to be about — `rule:core-classes/db-capabilities`, `rule:core-api/shape-parameter`, `rule:core-api/shape-arms-are-disjoint` and `rule:core-api/shape-flattens-at-the-abi`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/db-capabilities` gives `open` two halves for the four drivers with an address:
// the `db.open` grant, then `rule:http-server/allow-url-pins-the-address`'s table against the resolved host. The
// SQLite arm has neither a host nor an address, so what stands in their place
// is the path — asked of `db.open` under `nvs_config`'s canonicalise-then-
// prefix rule, and then of `fs.read` and `fs.write`, which is § 3's own
// "additionally needs" in as many words.
//
// The grant below covers a host and this call names a file, so the two cannot
// match and the refusal says which capability and which argument. That is the
// whole point: a host-shaped grant is not a licence to open a local file, and
// an operator who wants one writes a path root instead.
try {
    var $db = Core\Db::open({driver: Core\Db\Driver::Sqlite, path: "secrets.db"});
    echo "opened", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// `rule:security/denial-is-a-runtime-error`: a denial is the world refusing a call, not the program dying.
echo "still running", "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# A grant written for the four drivers that have a host. Nothing here is a path
# root, so the SQLite arm reaches none of it.
[capabilities.db]
open = ["*.tenants.internal"]
--EXPECTF--
Core\Db::open needs the capability `db.open` for %s%esecrets.db, which is not granted
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.db]`
still running
